            <!-- Season -->
            <div class="dest-section" data-reveal>
              <p class="kicker">When to Visit</p>
              <h2><em>Season-wise</em> visit expectations.</h2>
              <p>Ladakh sees its peak window from mid-June through September, when roads from Manali and Srinagar are open. The shoulder season (May, early October) is increasingly preferred for quieter trails. Deep winter — December to February — is reserved for the Chadar Trek and the most seasoned travellers.</p>
              <div class="dest-feature-list">
                <div class="dest-feature">
                  <div class="ico">☀</div>
                  <div>
                    <h5>Summer · Jun – Sep</h5>
                    <p>Peak season. Roads open, monasteries lively, 15–25°C days. Book well in advance.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="dest-feature">
                  <div class="ico">❄</div>
                  <div>
                    <h5>Winter · Dec – Feb</h5>
                    <p>Frozen rivers, Chadar Trek (subject to safety approvals), -20°C nights. Specialised gear required.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="dest-feature">
                  <div class="ico">✦</div>
                  <div>
                    <h5>Shoulder · May, Oct</h5>
                    <p>Editor's choice. Quieter trails, golden light, sharper air. Some passes may still be opening or closing.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="dest-feature">
                  <div class="ico">⛅</div>
                  <div>
                    <h5>Late Autumn · Nov</h5>
                    <p>Pangong and Tso Moriri start to freeze. Stark beauty, low traffic, hotel rates softening.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- How to reach -->
            <div class="dest-section" data-reveal>
              <p class="kicker">Getting There</p>
              <h2><em>How</em> to reach.</h2>
              <p>Three primary routes — by air to Leh's Kushok Bakula Rimpochee Airport (year-round, direct from Delhi), or two seasonal road journeys: the Manali-Leh highway (Jun–Sep) crossing five mountain passes, and the older Srinagar-Leh route (May–Oct) via Kargil. Each route is a journey in itself, and the choice shapes the trip.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- Stay options -->
            <div class="dest-section" data-reveal>
              <p class="kicker">Where to Stay</p>
              <h2>Stay <em>options</em>.</h2>
              <p>From luxury riverside camps in Nubra to homestays in Sumda Chenmo, the spectrum is wider than it appears. Our verified partners include three categories: heritage homestays under village families, mid-range hotels in Leh, and the premium camps that have built quietly remarkable reputations across the Indus Valley.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- Travel options -->
            <div class="dest-section" data-reveal>
              <p class="kicker">Getting Around</p>
              <h2>Travel <em>options</em>.</h2>
              <p>The classic mode is the Royal Enfield, but shared taxis cover most circuits well, and private SUV hires are the practical choice for first-timers acclimatising to altitude. We recommend at least 48 hours in Leh before any high-pass attempt.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- Activities -->
            <div class="dest-section" data-reveal>
              <p class="kicker">What to Do</p>
              <h2>Activities <em>to do</em>.</h2>
              <p>Beyond the obvious — Pangong, Nubra, Khardung La — there are quieter pleasures. Sunrise prayer at Thiksey. The market in Leh on a Saturday morning. The walk to Shanti Stupa just before dusk. A homestay dinner in Turtuk, where the Indus turns south.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- Landmarks -->
            <div class="dest-section" data-reveal>
              <p class="kicker">Places of Note</p>
              <h2>Landmarks <em>to visit</em>.</h2>
              <p>Hemis Monastery for its mask festival, Diskit for its 32-metre Maitreya Buddha overlooking the Shyok valley, Lamayuru for the moonscape that surrounds it, Pangong Lake for the silence it produces in everyone who stands beside it.</p>
            </div>

